-### Coverage floor — deliberately deferred
-
-**v0.3 does not set a numeric coverage floor** and does not use `--cov-fail-under`. Before **Amendment 1 at Session 15**, the team will generate and inspect a real `pytest-cov` report from its own implemented code, identify untested behavior, and then choose and record a justified numerical minimum in **Amendment 1**. Until then, producing coverage evidence is required when tests run; meeting an invented percentage is not.
+### Coverage floor — Amendment 1 proposal: 60% statement coverage
+
+**Commitment proposed for the rest of the semester:** at least **60.00% aggregate
+statement (line) coverage** of the **Team1 first-party Python/Django application
+runtime**, including ticket models, views/endpoints, forms/serializers, services,
+permission and validation logic, URLs, project configuration and entry points.
+This is an application-wide floor, not a whole-repository or domain-only floor,
+and not a per-file minimum. Branch coverage has no numeric commitment here.
+
+Exclude tests, documentation helpers under tools/docs/, disposable gate fixtures,
+third-party/virtual-environment code, generated migration files and non-Python
+assets. Include manually written migration logic. Never omit difficult application
+files to make the percentage pass. Before measurement, record the actual package
+paths and full included-file inventory in docs/COVERAGE_SCOPE.md; no product paths
+are invented by this proposal. Review every scope/configuration change in a PR.
+
+Measure 100 × covered executable statements / total executable statements across
+that scope, weighted by statement count rather than averaging file percentages.
+Configure coverage to include all in-scope modules, including unimported modules,
+and disable branch measurement for this metric. Use precision=2. Missing reports,
+an empty application denominator, failed tests, or zero collected product tests
+do not count as meeting the floor. Documentation-only CI must label product
+coverage NOT APPLICABLE; it must not report a fabricated 0%, 60%, or 100% result.
+
+Once the actual source and reviewed coverage configuration exist, add
+--cov-config=.coveragerc --cov-fail-under=60 to the section 8 pytest command in
+both local instructions and CI. Configure .coveragerc source entries with the
+actual runtime packages and all in-scope root entry points; use the same reviewed
+omissions locally and in CI. Verify that every in-scope source file appears in
+the report, including never-imported files. The existing --cov=. command alone
+does not establish the intended scope. Do not claim this documentation patch
+implements or verifies the CI threshold. Implementation remains a blocking
+follow-up before claiming product coverage compliance.
+
+Retain the commit, exact command, environment, test pass/fail/skip counts, complete
+per-file terminal table and TOTAL, coverage.xml, scope inventory and CI run link.
+Reviewer checks both the percentage and uncovered behavior. Before approving a
+product PR, all applicable tests must pass and this scoped floor must be met.
+Ticket access/visibility, lifecycle transitions, invalid input and persistence
+still require behavior checks under sections 2 and 7; aggregate coverage cannot
+substitute for them. Review skips so critical acceptance checks are not skipped.
+
+At each sprint review, check coverage and omissions. Schedule test work early
+and reduce feature scope when absences or deadlines threaten compliance. Do not
+silently waive or lower the floor to clear a failing PR. Policy revisions require
+the existing adoption procedure and a recorded rationale and course compliance.
+
+Rationale A2 (numeric choice and scope): 60% is a deliberately modest planning
+judgment under the user's stated lack of application/test access. It limits the
+aggregate uncovered allowance to roughly 40% while leaving room for integration
+work and uneven student availability. It is not an industry requirement, proof
+of adequate quality, or a guarantee that the unknown baseline already meets it.
+Application-wide scope captures integration code that a narrow domain-only floor
+could omit; excluding tests/helpers prevents those from inflating product results.
+Critical behavior checks mitigate, but do not eliminate, the limits of a modest
+aggregate line metric. The team must validate feasibility against its real report.
+
+Rationale A3 (evidence and enforcement): At saved Team1 commit
+ba34d08711ff64aa4cfe484a16013ed2d6971bb4, the CI artifact's scope.json says
+application=false with empty sources/manage lists, and application-tests.txt says
+no product tests or product coverage passed. Therefore the available baseline is
+unavailable, not zero. Personal-repository PR #18 explicitly identifies its 89%
+as separate prototype coverage and cannot justify this floor. Scope inventory,
+matching local/CI configuration, missing-report failures and retained evidence
+make the proposed number auditable without claiming measurements that do not exist.
+
+**Assignment limitation:** This proposal replaces v0.3's deferral with a numeric
+policy recommendation, but cannot satisfy the rubric's actual-team-coverage-report
+justification until that report is obtained. Do not present 60% as empirically
+derived or claim adoption, enforcement, passing application tests, or submission.
 
